The function swaps the contents of the files referenced by and in
an atomic fashion.  That is, all concurrent processes will either
see the pre‐exchanged state or the post‐exchanged state; they can
never  see  the  files in an inconsistent state.  The data in all
forks is swapped in this way.  The  parameter  lets  you  control
specific aspects of the function’s behaviour.  Open file descrip‐
tors follow the swapped data.  Thus, a descriptor that previously
referenced  will now reference the data that’s accessible via and
vice versa.  In general, the file attributes (metadata)  are  not
exchanged.   Specifically, the object identifier attributes (that
is, the and attributes  as  defined  by  the  function)  are  not
swapped.  An exception to this general rule is that the modifica‐
tion  time  attribute  ( ) is swapped.  When combined, these fea‐
tures allow you to implement a ’safe save’ function that does not
break references to the file (for example, aliases).   You  first
save  the  new contents to a temporary file and then exchange the
data of the original file and the temporary.  Programs that  ref‐
erence  the file via an object identifier will continue to refer‐
ence the original file, but now it has the  new  data.   WARNING:
